CloudBurrow patch 16 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1133, #1057, #1103).

The bug: Analyzer.parseScript (internal/analyzer.go) took its parser options
from AnalyzerOptions.GetParserOptions, which returns a new C++
ParserOptions, holding the arena and string pool the parse tree is built in,
as a go-googlesql handle with no finalizer. Nothing freed it, so each
statement parsed leaked its parse tree in the engine's WebAssembly memory,
which never shrinks: every query job, and each of the queries the emulator
runs on its own metadata at every request. Measured on the patched emulator
built natively on an Apple silicon Mac, 3,500 jobs.query calls of a 10-row
SELECT: the WebAssembly memory went from 70 to 279 MiB and the process from
159 to 989 MiB; with the patch, over 3,000 such calls, 70 MiB and 297 to
301 MiB. The options are now made with NewParserOptions2 from the analyzer's
language options, a handle go-googlesql frees by finalizer once no
ParserOutput (each keeps its options reachable) is left.

